  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nThe primary forecast challenge remains thunderstorm timing and\nstrength through midweek with a complex of severe thunderstorms\npossible mid/late Tuesday morning into early Tuesday afternoon.\n\nA very moist airmass is in place with dewpoints in the mid/upper\n70s with a few locations tagging 80. Isolated thunderstorms have\npersisted across eastern IA and far northwest IL the past few \nhours but have been slow to strengthen. However, the expectation\nis for this activity to continue moving east and to slowly \nincrease in coverage and intensity. As it taps into the high \ninstability in place, some of these storms may become severe \nacross northern IL and far northwest IN and persist through mid \nevening. In addition to the severe threat, torrential rain and \nlocalized flooding will also be possible though there remains \nsome uncertainty for how widespread any flooding may become. \nConsideration was given for a flood watch but have opted to hold\noff for now. Much of the guidance has trended drier for the \nlate evening and overnight hours tonight and have followed this \ntrend with this forecast though some showers and isolated \nthunderstorms may linger.\n\nFocus then shifts to a rather consistent feature in most of the\nmodels for a complex of severe thunderstorms to develop over\nnortheast IA early Tuesday morning and race southeast across the\nlocal area from mid morning Tuesday through early Tuesday\nafternoon. Some of the cam solutions are quite concerning with a\nline of severe thunderstorms moving across a large part of the\ncwa. The updated SPC Day 2 outlook has included a level 3 of 5\nsevere threat for most of the area and given all of these trends\nhave mentioned wind gusts to 80 mph possible and if these storm\nmature as shown on some of the cams, pockets of higher gusts \nmay materialize. Based on these trends, have also included \nsevere mention to the grids and zone forecast. Once these \nstorms exit to the east, there should be a lull in the \nprecipitation chances from mid/late Tuesday afternoon through \nat least the early overnight hours Wednesday morning.\n\nThere is another wave that may move across the region  \nWednesday morning with another potential severe thunderstorm \ncomplex near or over the local area. Quite a bit of uncertainty\nfor this time period, but will need to be monitored closely. In\naddition, all of these periods of heavy/torrential rain will\nallow each following wave/complex to potentially produce \nflooding more quickly and over larger areas.\n\nThe pattern is now looking quite active into this weekend, \nthough there remains quite a bit of uncertainty for timing and \nlocation as a series of high pressure systems and cold fronts \nmove across the upper midwest and northern Great Lakes. How fast\nthese move, how far south they push, etc. will have large \nimpacts on when thunderstorms would be most likely across the \nlocal area. cms",
